variable "tenant_id" {
  type        = string
  description = "Entra tenant. Read from `az account show`; never committed."
}

variable "subscription_id" {
  type        = string
  description = "sub-lab-dev. Everything this week creates lands here."
}

variable "location" {
  type        = string
  default     = "southcentralus"
  description = "Region for everything this week creates."
}

variable "ssh_public_key_path" {
  type        = string
  default     = "~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub"
  description = <<-EOT
    Path to an EXISTING SSH public key. Azure requires a key or a password on a
    Linux VM, and a password is not an option in this lab. The key is not
    generated by Terraform on purpose: a generated key puts its private half in
    state, which is exactly the kind of secret this lab refuses to create.
  EOT
}

variable "vm_size" {
  type        = string
  default     = "Standard_D2als_v7"
  description = <<-EOT
    The Prometheus host. 2 vCPU / 4 GB, far more than this scrapes, but the
    smallest size that holds Prometheus without becoming an exercise in tuning.
    Billed per hour, so the week is torn down.

    Two things decided this value and neither was obvious:

      * `Standard_B2s` (v1) is not offered in southcentralus at all. It fails
        with `SkuNotAvailable ... Capacity Restrictions`, which reads like a
        transient capacity problem and is not: the SKU simply is not in the
        region's list.
      * Every v2 B-series family -- Basv2, Bsv2, Bpsv2 -- has a vCPU quota of
        ZERO on this subscription. `az vm list-skus` reports them as
        unrestricted, because SKU restrictions and quota are different things
        and quota is only checked at create time.
      * The sizes carrying a `p` are ARM64. cloud-init downloads the
        linux-amd64 Prometheus, so an ARM size boots fine and then cannot
        start Prometheus.

    `Standard_D2als_v7` clears all three: offered in the region, family quota
    10, AMD x64. Total Regional vCPUs on this subscription is 10, so two is
    comfortable.
  EOT
}

variable "prometheus_version" {
  type        = string
  default     = "3.5.0"
  description = <<-EOT
    Downloaded in cloud-init rather than installed from the distro, because the
    version matters: Microsoft documents 2.45+ for user-assigned managed
    identity remote-write and 3.5.0+ for system-assigned. Pinning it means the
    week can state which version it proved.
  EOT
}

variable "grafana_sku" {
  type        = string
  default     = "Standard"
  description = <<-EOT
    The only value azurerm accepts. Essential exists in Azure and is cheaper -
    no hourly node charge - but it is Preview and the provider cannot express
    it, so reaching it would mean azapi for a preview SKU. Standard is GA, and
    a new instance is free for 30 days (one per subscription).
  EOT
}

variable "alert_up_threshold" {
  type        = number
  default     = 0
  description = <<-EOT
    The alert rule fires when `up{job="prometheus"}` exceeds this. `up` is 1
    when the scrape succeeded and 0 when it did not, so a threshold of 0 means
    "fire while Prometheus is reporting at all".

    That is deliberately trivial. The claim this week makes is that a rule
    written in Terraform becomes a rule Azure evaluates against remote-written
    data - not that the threshold is a sensible SLO.
  EOT
}
